#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209502} :activity/id #uuid "47de0bc5-4997-5b2e-8cab-b46fd4d7b3c1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048394 :topic/id #uuid "9bb1a756-2d08-54f2-a8b6-a335801f9dc8" :topic/math-academy-id 5997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209887 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X$ is a discrete random variable, the expected value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{x} x\\cdot P(X = x)\n$$\n\nThere is an alternative method for computing $\\textrm{E}[X]$, which allows us to compute the expected value of a random variable by conditioning on another variable.\n\nSuppose another discrete random variable $Y$ takes on a set of possible values. If we know\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]\n$$\n\nfor each value $y$, then the **law of total expectation** states\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nConceptually, this tells us that $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is just a weighted average of the conditional expectations\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]\n$$\n\nwhere each weight is the probability\n\n$$\nP(Y = y)\n$$\n\nIn many problems, directly computing $\\textrm{E}[X]$ from the definition can be difficult. It turns out that breaking $\\textrm{E}[X]$ up into conditional pieces often simplifies things. The law of total expectation is especially useful when $X$ depends on an underlying condition $Y$, and each conditional case is easier to analyze than the full distribution of $X$.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice with a few examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "48593a4b-9b04-583a-b9c6-5d781d9c5d4c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12912 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4ca67c7d-008b-5867-a495-27c33923a7f3" :step/math-academy-id 448840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209888 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ with probabilities $0.4$ and $0.6$, respectively. Suppose we are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n- $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\alpha] = 7$\n- $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\beta] = 3$\n\nLet's compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the law of total expectation.\n\nThe law of total expectation states\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]= \\sum_y \\textrm{E} [X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y = \\alpha$ with probability $0.4$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\alpha] = 7$\n- $Y = \\beta$ with probability $0.6$, and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = \\beta] = 3$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[X∣Y = α] \\cdot P(Y = α) + E[X∣Y = β] \\cdot P(Y = β) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 0.4 + 3 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 2.8 + 1.8 \\\\\n&= 4.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d728e35d-7ac2-5df5-a542-66e724725c43" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12913 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of the Total Expectation"} :step/id #uuid "e48c6b64-85c3-54f7-b599-8bad2d53d91b" :step/math-academy-id 448841 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208276 :question/id #uuid "84213a67-69f3-5f2a-a3b0-f0309257e545" :question/math-academy-id "e-18338"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048872}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208941}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"} :step/id #uuid "a48363be-74c4-5103-b7f2-577e639edd7c" :step/math-academy-id 448842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208277 :question/id #uuid "0a8fcaef-ae9f-5e55-acb1-58a34cfa2894" :question/math-academy-id "e-18339"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208943}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"} :step/id #uuid "4fc63549-cf8b-5c8b-a9d9-722a7f80d6f7" :step/math-academy-id 448845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209885 :tutorial/content "We’ve seen that the law of total expectation allows us to compute the expected value of a random variable $X$ by conditioning on another variable $Y$. This idea extends naturally to a more general and elegant identity called the **tower property**:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this may look abstract, so let’s unpack what it means.\n\nThe inner term $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]$ is itself a *random variable*—its value depends on the outcome of $Y$. The outer expectation then takes the average of that conditional expectation over all possible values of $Y$.\n\nIn other words, we are averaging $X$ in two stages:\n\n- Within each scenario (for each possible value of $Y$), we find the expected value of $X$.\n- Across all scenarios, we take the weighted average of those conditional expectations, where the weights are the probabilities of each $Y$.\n\nLet’s explore a few examples to build some intuition." :tutorial/id #uuid "bc773ad5-ce02-5722-96a2-08ef2c5ac53d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12910 :tutorial/title "The Tower Property"} :step/id #uuid "9f568acb-64b8-5742-9e38-0a387a810b56" :step/math-academy-id 448848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208278 :question/id #uuid "4ccdabba-d496-575e-99d6-ad102aca2811" :question/math-academy-id "e-18340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208947}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"} :step/id #uuid "97f215d4-870d-5144-a7a0-5c0bf9e5c6e3" :step/math-academy-id 448849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208279 :question/id #uuid "f6e9b398-63d8-5e0a-8333-7d8971e6d261" :question/math-academy-id "e-18341"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050169}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208946}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"} :step/id #uuid "c7f91615-6efd-5943-936c-fc011cd4ccc1" :step/math-academy-id 448852 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209886 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $Y$ takes only two values $y_1$ and $y_2$. We want to show that the *law of total expectation* holds in this simple case:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}[X] = \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y = y_1] \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y = y_2] \\cdot P(Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of expectation, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x)\n$$\n\nNext, we express\n\n$$\nP(X = x)\n$$\n\nusing the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2) \\cdot P(Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the expectation formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\left(P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2) \\cdot P(Y = y_2) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now distribute the sum across the two terms\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = P(Y = y_1) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y_1) + P(Y = y_2) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y_2)\n$$\n\nBut each of the sums above is just a conditional expectation according to the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(x)x \\cdot P(X = x∣Y = y_{1}) &= E[X∣Y = y_{1}] \\\\\n∑_(x)x \\cdot P(X = x∣Y = y_{2}) &= E[X∣Y = y_{2}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y_1] \\cdot P(Y = y_1) + \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y_2] \\cdot P(Y = y_2)}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the more general case, where $Y$ takes more than two values." :tutorial/id #uuid "d272a3af-24ad-528c-8329-ac3ffd9fcca3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12911 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Law of Total Expectation"} :step/id #uuid "debbbe9e-b2bd-51ea-837a-ea01ca326804" :step/math-academy-id 448855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209889 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables. Now, we want to prove that the tower property holds:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of expectation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the law of total probability to express\n\n$$\nP(X = x)\n$$\n\nin terms of $Y$. Let $S_Y$ denote the support of $Y$. Then, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} P(X = x \\mid Y = y) \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expectation formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_x x \\left(\\sum_{y \\in S_Y} P(X = x \\mid Y = y) \\cdot P(Y = y) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now switch the order of summation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} P(Y = y) \\cdot \\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y)\n$$\n\nEach inner sum is just the conditional expectation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum_x x \\cdot P(X = x \\mid Y = y) = \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y]\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nBut this expression is just the definition of $\\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]$ for discrete $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]] = \\sum_{y \\in S_Y} \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "166484c1-896c-5706-ad16-7343be405baf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12914 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Tower Property"} :step/id #uuid "851cc24c-2ebe-53f8-9797-8effa5e877f5" :step/math-academy-id 448856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209955 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y] = \\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sqrt3\n$$\n\nlet's compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$.\n\nThis is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[\\sqrt{3}Y + 1] \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot E[Y] + E[1] \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8627a2e4-c1aa-5283-8634-38141c143520" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13296 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of the Tower Property"} :step/id #uuid "e2cd82f1-e689-5d93-88a2-dd6762a02ce1" :step/math-academy-id 462656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209507}}] :activity/title "The Law of Total Expectation for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186208871}]}
